<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulama</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ulama are the community of Muslim religious scholars, trained in Islamic law, theology and scripture. They staffed courts, schools and mosques and often mediated between rulers and subjects. The forced conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi'ism</a> created an expanding role for the clerical establishment, and the ulama also feature in the governance of the Seljuk and Zand states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulama.jpg" alt="Ulama" width="320" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zereshk · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maqamat_hariri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
